From 096b40b873e2d99d62382c387072e458dc5a1c4f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 03:24:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] grep-searcher: document the three search strategies

glue.rs defines ReadByLine, SliceByLine and MultiLine but did not explain
how they differ. Add a module overview contrasting them (streaming reader
with a rolling buffer; single in-memory slice, line-oriented; single slice
with a matcher that spans lines) and doc comments on each struct, on
ReadByLine::fill (the roll/refill step), and on MultiLine::sink (the
adjacent-match coalescing that keeps a line from being reported twice).

Comment-only: no code changes.
---
 crates/searcher/src/searcher/glue.rs | 55 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 55 insertions(+)

diff --git a/crates/searcher/src/searcher/glue.rs b/crates/searcher/src/searcher/glue.rs
index 006afad..b74e0f3 100644
--- a/crates/searcher/src/searcher/glue.rs
+++ b/crates/searcher/src/searcher/glue.rs
@@ -1,3 +1,28 @@
+/*!
+The concrete search strategies that drive the shared [`Core`] engine.
+
+Every search does the same bookkeeping (that is [`Core`]'s job — see
+`core.rs`); these three types differ only in where the bytes come from and how
+matches are located. The `Searcher` picks one based on the input and the
+matcher:
+
+* [`ReadByLine`] — line-oriented search over an arbitrary streaming
+  [`std::io::Read`] source (e.g. stdin, or a file not being memory-mapped).
+  Because the whole input need not fit in memory, it drives a fixed-size
+  rolling [`LineBufferReader`]: fill the buffer, search it, roll it forward,
+  repeat. Only used when the matcher is *not* multi-line.
+* [`SliceByLine`] — line-oriented search over a single in-memory `&[u8]` (a
+  memory-mapped file, or a fully buffered stream). The entire haystack is
+  present at once, so there is no rolling. Also non-multi-line.
+* [`MultiLine`] — search over a single in-memory `&[u8]` with a matcher that
+  can match *across* line boundaries. Matches are found with the general
+  `Matcher::find` (not by stepping line by line), then snapped out to whole
+  lines and grouped so overlapping/adjacent matches are reported once.
+
+[`Core`]: crate::searcher::core::Core
+[`LineBufferReader`]: crate::line_buffer::LineBufferReader
+*/
+
 use grep_matcher::Matcher;
 
 use crate::{
@@ -7,6 +32,10 @@ use crate::{
     sink::{Sink, SinkError},
 };
 
